WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.540
Ben Wattenberg: Hello, I’m Ben Wattenberg.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.280
On this edition of “Think Tank,” we will
talk one on one with social critic and economist

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.690
Thomas Sowell of the Hoover Institution.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:18.230
Tom Sowell’s new book, “The Vision of
the Anointed,” argues that the assumptions

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:25.320
and beliefs of America’s liberal elite have
created 30 years of disaster in education,

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.080
crime, and welfare policy.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.630
What are these assumptions?

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:31.099
Are they wrong?

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:32.949
Where are they leading us?

00:00:32.949 --> 00:00:47.350
A conversation with Thomas Sowell, this week
on “Think Tank.”

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:58.869
Joining us today is Thomas Sowell, a senior
fellow at the Hoover Institution at Stanford

00:00:58.869 --> 00:01:00.280
University.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:05.619
He is the author of many books that have cast
a critical eye at American society, including

00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:10.140
“Inside American Education” and “Race
and Culture.”

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.240
His latest book, “The Vision of the Anointed:
Self-Congratulation as the Basis for Social

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:21.701
Policy,” has been called a broadside against
the received wisdom of America’s elite liberal

00:01:21.701 --> 00:01:24.509
intelligentsia.

00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:30.159
Tom Sowell, why don’t you start out and
tell us what “The Vision of the Anointed”

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:31.159
is about.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:32.159
What is it?

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:35.201
Tom Sowell: Well, it’s, one, a vision that
the problems that we see in the world are

00:01:35.201 --> 00:01:38.500
due to the fact that other people are just
not as bright or as compassionate as they

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.880
are and that there are all these solutions
out there waiting to be discovered and they

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.490
have them and that these solutions are to
be imposed upon the rest of us by the power

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:51.469
of government through taxation or in other
ways.

00:01:51.469 --> 00:01:57.600
And what’s really crucial about it is that
their passion is so much greater than the

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.869
passion on the other side, largely because
what they have involved is more.

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:02.749
Ben Wattenberg: Who is they?

00:02:02.749 --> 00:02:09.140
Tom Sowell: Oh, the media elite, the academic
elite, political elites.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:14.030
And the reason we talk about their vision,
even though they obviously in their opinions,

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:20.510
is that the basic set of underlying assumptions
about the world are very similar.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:25.540
And because these assumptions are the prevailing
assumptions, the need to find evidence for

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:28.500
them or to offer proof is much less.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.710
If something happens, they’ll explain it
in a way which will fit that vision.

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:38.890
For example, when they find that prenatal
care is less among blacks than among whites

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:43.880
and that infant mortality rates are higher,
they immediately assume this is because of

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:47.380
society’s neglect, and therefore if only
the government will step in and provide more

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.220
prenatal care, then that problem solves itself.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.450
But in reality, other groups have even less
prenatal care than blacks and don’t have

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:58.010
any more infant mortality than whites.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:02.070
But they don’t ever get to that second stage
because once they’ve seen something that

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:05.820
fits their conception of how the world works,
that’s sort of the end of it.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:10.340
Ben Wattenberg: Let me go back to that idea
of who the they is.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.270
I mean, in your cosmology, are these liberals?

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:14.270
Is that what they are?

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:15.720
Tom Sowell: Yes.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:19.530
The New York Times, The Washington Post, Harvard,
Stanford.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.650
Oh, the Edward Kennedys, the — all the usual
suspects.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:23.930
Ben Wattenberg: Let me —

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:28.740
Tom Sowell: But it’s more than those particular
people because this mindset goes back at least

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:29.740
200 years.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:31.890
Ben Wattenberg: Who does it start with?

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:35.002
Tom Sowell: I don’t know where it starts,
but you could find it in the 18th century.

00:03:35.002 --> 00:03:41.000
If you read William Godwin, “Inquiry Concerning
Political Justice,” in 1793, you have the

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.300
whole vision laid out just as it was in the
1960s.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.510
But the 1960s were a crucial point because
that’s when this vision became dominant.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:52.100
Ben Wattenberg: This sort of arrogant vision
that we know best?

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:53.100
Tom Sowell: Oh, yes.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:56.770
Ben Wattenberg: And don’t even have to subject
it to normal forms of proof?

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:57.770
Tom Sowell: Oh, absolutely not.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:58.770
Absolutely not.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:05.120
Ben Wattenberg: Now, let me read you something
that Speaker Gingrich wrote in his book “To

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.120
Renew America.”

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:15.260
He said, “Since 1965, there has been a calculated
effort by cultural elites to discredit this

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:21.969
civilization and replace it with a culture
of irresponsibility that is incompatible with

00:04:21.969 --> 00:04:24.419
American freedoms as we have known them.”

00:04:24.419 --> 00:04:27.999
Do you buy that?

00:04:27.999 --> 00:04:29.310
Is that basically what you’re —

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.319
Tom Sowell: Yes, although I would limit to
those who are sort of at the top of this kind

00:04:33.319 --> 00:04:36.999
of thing, because further down the people
believe things because so many others believe

00:04:36.999 --> 00:04:38.840
them because they’re in the air and so on.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:43.510
Ben Wattenberg: You know, I was particularly
interested in — what he said was that it

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:46.520
is “a calculated effort.”

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:53.069
Now, do you believe that the people, “the
anointed,” as you call them, are sitting

00:04:53.069 --> 00:04:58.560
around in a conspiratorial mode calculating,
as Newt Gingrich says, and saying, “Here’s

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.479
what we’re going to do”?

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:04.479
Or do you just think they’re wrong and this
is what they believe and they have this certain

00:05:04.479 --> 00:05:05.479
arrogance about it?

00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:06.560
Because those are two very different ideas,
you know.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.689
Tom Sowell: Well, this is why I made the distinction
between the leaders and the others.

00:05:09.689 --> 00:05:14.270
I mean, I think that when people say things
like, “More American wives are battered

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:18.810
on Super Bowl Sunday” — you see that — “than
any other time of the year,” and there’s

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:26.499
not a speck of evidence for that, that is
calculated because they — oh, I mean there

00:05:26.499 --> 00:05:30.539
is no data that could even be misinterpreted
that way; in other words, because there is

00:05:30.539 --> 00:05:32.580
no data, period.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.090
And so, yes.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:39.660
But I think that 99 percent of the people
who believe it are not calculating.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:43.900
I think one of the reasons that it flies without
even being challenged for evidence is that

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:47.990
there is a certain vision of how the world
is, and in that world, men are oppressing

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:48.990
women.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.729
And therefore when you say something like
this, it fits the vision, and that’s the

00:05:51.729 --> 00:05:52.729
end of it.

00:05:52.729 --> 00:05:54.230
There’s no demand for evidence.

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:59.029
Ben Wattenberg: You have in your book sort
of a series toward the beginning of how the

00:05:59.029 --> 00:06:03.449
actual process works of forming one of these
ideas —

00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:04.449
Tom Sowell: Yes.

00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:05.569
Ben Wattenberg: — and selling it and rejecting
the proof.

00:06:05.569 --> 00:06:08.660
Maybe you could just kind of march us through
this as a model of it.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:11.110
Tom Sowell: All right, there’s a four-stage
pattern.

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:14.930
And in the first stage is what I call the
crisis.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:18.949
And so we’re hyped to believe that something
is a terrible crisis for which something must

00:06:18.949 --> 00:06:20.939
be done.

00:06:20.939 --> 00:06:24.580
And what was fascinating to me in doing the
research for this book is that very often

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:29.229
the thing that’s said to be in crisis has
often been getting better for years on end,

00:06:29.229 --> 00:06:31.169
but that gets ignored.

00:06:31.169 --> 00:06:32.190
In the second stage —

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:36.529
Ben Wattenberg: For example, infant mortality,
to use one of the things you were talking

00:06:36.529 --> 00:06:37.529
about before.

00:06:37.529 --> 00:06:42.039
Tom Sowell: Well, I’m thinking about teenage
pregnancy and venereal disease.

00:06:42.039 --> 00:06:43.740
Those things were getting better.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.280
Teenage pregnancy was going down for more
than a decade before sex education was introduced.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:54.559
Venereal disease, syphilis in the 1960s had
only half the incidence that it had in 1950.

00:06:54.559 --> 00:06:59.449
So all of these things are going down, yet
we’re said to need sex education to deal

00:06:59.449 --> 00:07:01.830
with this crisis which has been manufactured.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:03.849
Again, this is where the calculated part comes
in.

00:07:03.849 --> 00:07:09.749
Now, 99 percent of the people who hear this
don’t know that, but the reason they accept

00:07:09.749 --> 00:07:13.750
it is because they also share the same vision,
and because this is consonant with that vision,

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:14.750
they don’t have to ask for evidence.

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:15.979
Ben Wattenberg: All right, so what’s stage
two?

00:07:15.979 --> 00:07:16.979
Tom Sowell: Stage two is —

00:07:16.979 --> 00:07:18.719
Ben Wattenberg: The first one is there’s
a crisis.

00:07:18.719 --> 00:07:19.719
Tom Sowell: Yes.

00:07:19.719 --> 00:07:22.400
Ben Wattenberg: They establish a crisis, usually
an artificial one.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.059
Tom Sowell: Yes.

00:07:24.059 --> 00:07:25.690
Then stage two is the solution.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:26.889
You have a solution for this crisis.

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:29.990
In this case, you have sex education in the
schools.

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:34.400
And then at that point, you say, “If we
do this, this will lead to beneficial result

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:38.960
A.” The critics say, “No, it will lead
to detrimental result Z.”

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:41.229
Stage three are the results.

00:07:41.229 --> 00:07:45.580
You put it in and directly you find detrimental
result Z — namely, venereal disease and

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:48.449
teenage pregnancy take off into the stratosphere.

00:07:48.449 --> 00:07:52.680
And then stage four is the fascinating part
in which they simply say, “No, that doesn’t

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:56.199
prove that this was a bad policy because there
are many factors.

00:07:56.199 --> 00:07:57.270
There is complexity.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.090
It’s simplistic to blame it on this.”

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:03.039
Well, they run through this routine on so
many different things, including crime.

00:08:03.039 --> 00:08:07.830
Similarly, they said, you know, in 1960, Judge
Baselon said we just desperately need to have

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:10.069
some kind of change in the criminal justice
system.

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:17.210
Now, in 1960, there were fewer murders than
there had been in 1950, 1940, or 1930.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:19.259
But again, that was completely ignored.

00:08:19.259 --> 00:08:22.379
And so now we have the revolution of the criminal
justice system.

00:08:22.379 --> 00:08:26.080
People say, “No, if you put these new things
in, there will be more crime than before.”

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.210
They put them in.

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:31.759
Almost instantly the declining crime rate
turns around and heads up again.

00:08:31.759 --> 00:08:34.880
And they say, “No, it’s simplistic to
blame this on this.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.840
The root cause is in the neglect of society,
and all the rest of it.”

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.220
So it’s heads I win, tails you lose.

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:49.440
Ben Wattenberg: So to go to that earliest
example that you gave, you think the increase

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.990
in venereal disease was caused by sex education?

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:55.120
Tom Sowell: I don’t have to say that.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:56.660
I don’t even have to believe that.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:57.660
All I have to say is that they —

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:58.660
Ben Wattenberg: But do you?

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:00.730
Tom Sowell: Oh, I think it’s hard to explain
otherwise.

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:08.139
I mean, you know, you don’t get social changes
that drastic in a few years without some particular

00:09:08.139 --> 00:09:09.139
cause.

00:09:09.139 --> 00:09:10.810
But the argument doesn’t depend on that
at all.

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:14.339
The point is, they created the crisis artificially.

00:09:14.339 --> 00:09:16.970
The evidence shows there was no crisis.

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:21.690
But they would not even subject it to any
empirical test.

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:25.060
If they want to show some other factor came
in and really caused this, I’m happy to

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:26.060
hear that.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:32.339
Ben Wattenberg: Why would a group of liberal
intellectuals or politicians or media stars

00:09:32.339 --> 00:09:40.589
or whatever, why would they sit around and
decide to dismember or dilute the criminal

00:09:40.589 --> 00:09:46.160
justice system if they thought in advance
that it would raise the amount of criminality?

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:48.630
Tom Sowell: Oh, they didn’t think that,
but the point is —

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:51.220
Ben Wattenberg: They just thought wrongly
that it would be — that it would help?

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:56.040
Tom Sowell: Yes, but it would also give them
an enormously larger role than they had before.

00:09:56.040 --> 00:10:00.240
I mean, a judge who just sits there and applies
the laws that have been passed by the legislature

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:01.600
has a very minor role.

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:07.320
But if he takes the expansive, judicial activist
role, then of course he’s on the leading

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:10.550
edge and he can look for the hosannas and
all the rest of it.

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:15.160
Ben Wattenberg: How does this play out in
the realm of something you have written about,

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:17.899
which is affirmative action?

00:10:17.899 --> 00:10:20.350
How does that process — what’s the 1,
2, 3, 4 on that?

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:26.449
Tom Sowell: I haven’t worked it out like
that, but certainly there is no interest whatever

00:10:26.449 --> 00:10:32.170
in finding out empirically whether things
have been made better or worse for minorities

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:33.680
as a result of this program.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.101
And in fact, if you bring up evidence, they’ll
say, “Ah, but things would have been even

00:10:38.101 --> 00:10:40.410
worse had we not done this.”

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:44.680
Similarly with the War on Poverty, you can
show how dependency on government was going

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:48.509
down, poverty was going down before this program
was ever put in.

00:10:48.509 --> 00:10:55.180
And within a few years, dependence on government
was going up, and after a few more years,

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:57.240
the absolute number of people in poverty was
going up.

00:10:57.240 --> 00:10:58.240
Ben Wattenberg: Yeah.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:02.160
I read that in your book, but the absolute
number of poverty is not the relevant data.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:07.379
The relevant data would be the rate of poverty,
and the rate of poverty, in fact, in the 19

00:11:07.379 --> 00:11:10.069
— I worked for President Johnson so we have
to establish that.

00:11:10.069 --> 00:11:11.069
Tom Sowell: Yes.

00:11:11.069 --> 00:11:15.750
Ben Wattenberg: You know, so I mean the line
we took was that during the 1960s, during

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:21.430
the Kennedy-Johnson administration, poverty
did in fact go down sharply.

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:25.250
And then the rate since then — not the absolute
number because the country has grown larger

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:29.360
— the rate has sort of bounced around at
about flat, somewhere between 12 and 15 percent.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.709
Tom Sowell: Yes, but don’t forget, this
was sold to the country not on the grounds

00:11:32.709 --> 00:11:36.550
that if you transferred money from A to B,
that B would have more money.

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:37.550
That was not the argument.

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:41.639
The argument was that dependency would be
reduced, that you would, quote, “invest

00:11:41.639 --> 00:11:45.870
in people,” as Bill Clinton is now saying
now that people have forgotten what was said

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:46.949
in the ’60s.

00:11:46.949 --> 00:11:50.579
This will then — you give them job training
and all those kinds of things, parenting skills,

00:11:50.579 --> 00:11:54.520
the whole bit, and this will then be an investment
that will pay off in the future because there

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:57.899
will be fewer people dependent upon the government
than there were before.

00:11:57.899 --> 00:12:01.200
And I go through a great number of people,
from John F. Kennedy to Lyndon Johnson, The

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:02.200
New York Times.

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:05.310
Again, all the usual suspects said all these
things.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:08.250
Ben Wattenberg: Lyndon Johnson was not a usual
suspect, Tom.

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:13.570
Tom Sowell: Well, he was the primary suspect,
all right.

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:15.889
But the fact is that was never tested.

00:12:15.889 --> 00:12:20.519
And when there were all these wonderful retrospectives
held down in Texas and other places about

00:12:20.519 --> 00:12:25.290
this, the first order of business is to evade
the criteria that they themselves set up when

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:26.410
they set this out.

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:31.800
And so no matter what happens, if it’s a
failure by the original criterion, then we

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:34.529
just find another criterion by which it will
be a success.

00:12:34.529 --> 00:12:37.750
Ben Wattenberg: Well, let’s just examine
that for a minute.

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:42.950
I have — a friend of mine from those old
White House days was listening to, again,

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:50.710
Gingrich, shortly after we had this great
Republican revolution in 1994 — great meaning

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:52.589
huge, not necessarily wonderful.

00:12:52.589 --> 00:12:54.370
We shall see about that.

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:59.110
And he kept hearing Newt Gingrich use the
word “opportunity” — “this conservative

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:02.899
opportunity society,” “we have to provide
opportunity.”

00:13:02.899 --> 00:13:06.730
And I was talking to him, and he said, “You
know, Gingrich uses that word ‘opportunity’

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:10.940
almost as much as Johnson did,” which is
what you’re saying, that that was the rhetoric.

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:17.030
It was called the Equal — OEO is the Office
of Economic Opportunity, and that’s what

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:18.030
it was.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:23.389
Now, so if liberals were talking about opportunity
and now conservatives are talking about opportunity,

00:13:23.389 --> 00:13:24.829
and I’m sure you’re for opportunity —

00:13:24.829 --> 00:13:28.029
Tom Sowell: All God’s children got opportunity.

00:13:28.029 --> 00:13:31.639
Ben Wattenberg: All God’s children got opportunity,
so what is your problem that liberals said

00:13:31.639 --> 00:13:34.050
we ought to create programs for opportunity?

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:39.410
And in point of fact, I mean just to — I
won’t say play devil’s advocate — a

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:45.930
lot of the things that came out of the Great
Society, building all those junior colleges

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:47.029
and community colleges —

00:13:47.029 --> 00:13:48.029
Tom Sowell: Oh, I would disagree entirely.

00:13:48.029 --> 00:13:49.421
That was a tragedy of the first magnitude.

00:13:49.421 --> 00:13:50.421
Ben Wattenberg: That was a tragedy?

00:13:50.421 --> 00:13:51.421
Tom Sowell: Yes.

00:13:51.421 --> 00:13:52.421
Ben Wattenberg: Why is that a tragedy?

00:13:52.421 --> 00:13:55.829
Tom Sowell: You have millions of people who
have absolutely no desire for an education

00:13:55.829 --> 00:14:00.240
using up billions of dollars of the taxpayers’
money and not only not getting an education

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:04.259
themselves, but making it more difficult to
give an education to those people who came

00:14:04.259 --> 00:14:05.720
to college with an idea of getting one.

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:08.089
Ben Wattenberg: Now, you say they have no
desire for an education.

00:14:08.089 --> 00:14:12.740
I mean, nobody is herding them into these
community colleges and into the junior colleges

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:13.740
and into the state universities.

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:14.740
Tom Sowell: Oh.

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:15.740
Oh, oh.

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:16.740
Ben Wattenberg: I mean, they have a desire,
obviously.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:18.430
Tom Sowell: No, they do not, obviously, because
lots of things go on in those places that

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:19.440
are not education.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:24.779
I mean, where else can you find so many young
people of the same age and opposite sex in

00:14:24.779 --> 00:14:28.470
one place, a nice convenient place to be?

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:33.959
But anyone who has taught in a lot of these
places, this ferocious desire for education

00:14:33.959 --> 00:14:36.279
as such is not terribly visible.

00:14:36.279 --> 00:14:40.060
And I’ve taught at places where we’ve
gotten, you know, the upper 10, 15 percent

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:46.220
of students, I mean UCLA and whatnot, and
neither I nor my colleagues found this great

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:48.080
desire for education as such.

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:51.529
They wanted to be in ivy-covered buildings
for four years in order to get more money

00:14:51.529 --> 00:14:54.220
when they graduated and have a good time while
they’re there.

00:14:54.220 --> 00:15:00.230
Ben Wattenberg: So you think the great American
ideal, which has really been shared by both

00:15:00.230 --> 00:15:08.019
parties and both ideologies over recent decades,
to allow more people to get into higher education,

00:15:08.019 --> 00:15:09.990
that that is a bankrupt idea?

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:15.980
Tom Sowell: Oh, to allow is one thing, but
to subsidize people at enormous cost with

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:19.750
no real sign that this is producing what we
intended it to do —

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:22.730
Ben Wattenberg: Well, when you say subsidize,
I mean talk about a junior college or community

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:23.730
college.

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:25.449
I guess that’s subsidizing.

00:15:25.449 --> 00:15:26.760
It’s below cost —

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:28.009
Tom Sowell: Sure.

00:15:28.009 --> 00:15:34.970
Ben Wattenberg: — tuition, but it does allow
kids who do not have the money to go to Amherst

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:38.649
or wherever to start a college education.

00:15:38.649 --> 00:15:39.649
I mean —

00:15:39.649 --> 00:15:42.009
Tom Sowell: People did that before the Great
Society.

00:15:42.009 --> 00:15:44.120
I did it before the Great Society.

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:46.060
My whole generation did it before the Great
Society.

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:53.660
Ben Wattenberg: You are saying that the great
humanitarian political impulses of the 1960s

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:56.930
have been, almost without exception, bad?

00:15:56.930 --> 00:16:02.720
Tom Sowell: Well, there were some good ones,
but I’m saying that the assumption that

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:07.699
on the education front — I mean, I would
defy you to find a large number of people

00:16:07.699 --> 00:16:11.300
who have actually taught these students who
really think that they’re out there thirsting

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:13.769
for knowledge.

00:16:13.769 --> 00:16:14.769
If you think —

00:16:14.769 --> 00:16:18.749
Ben Wattenberg: Suppose they are thirsting
for a better job, and we have set up a society

00:16:18.749 --> 00:16:22.019
where you have to be credentialed with a certain
amount of college.

00:16:22.019 --> 00:16:24.569
So aren’t they able to get a better job
because of their credentials?

00:16:24.569 --> 00:16:25.569
Tom Sowell: No.

00:16:25.569 --> 00:16:26.850
This is the fallacy of composition.

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:32.089
You know, if one person stands up in the stadium,
he sees the game better, but if they all stand

00:16:32.089 --> 00:16:34.569
up, they don’t all see the game better.

00:16:34.569 --> 00:16:38.089
As long as — you know, if you have a degree
and the other guy doesn’t, then you get

00:16:38.089 --> 00:16:39.269
ahead of him in the employment line.

00:16:39.269 --> 00:16:42.829
But we’re not going to all get ahead of
each other in the employment line by all getting

00:16:42.829 --> 00:16:43.829
degrees.

00:16:43.829 --> 00:16:50.709
Ben Wattenberg: So this whole idea that I
guess again both liberals and conservatives

00:16:50.709 --> 00:16:57.161
are saying is that, at this particular moment,
1995, we have to get more people into the

00:16:57.161 --> 00:16:59.889
education system because that’s the way
to compete.

00:16:59.889 --> 00:17:05.190
And we look at the data, and we see that the
people with more education are earning more

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:08.490
money than ever before relative to the people
with less education.

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:10.890
That’s all a fallacy of everybody standing
up in the stadium?

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:14.960
Tom Sowell: People who fly on the Concorde
— kids who have flown on the Concorde undoubtedly

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:18.020
will make more money than people who — kids
who have only gone on buses.

00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:20.900
That does not mean if we put a lot of people
on the Concorde, we’re going to raise the

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:22.240
national income.

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:25.439
Ben Wattenberg: We would increase the revenues
of the Concorde.

00:17:25.439 --> 00:17:29.030
Tom Sowell: Yes, which they desperately need,
of course, but that’s another story.

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:38.210
Ben Wattenberg: All right, and just to review
the bidding, you date the full-blown nature

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:41.630
of this situation sort of in the 1960s.

00:17:41.630 --> 00:17:42.630
Is that right?

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:43.630
Tom Sowell: Yes, yes.

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:44.919
Ben Wattenberg: I mean, and that’s — everyone
on the conservative side starts — I mean

00:17:44.919 --> 00:17:46.580
“genesis” is mid-1960s.

00:17:46.580 --> 00:17:49.090
That’s when the world started.

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:52.669
I mean, and I’m not arguing with that necessarily.

00:17:52.669 --> 00:17:53.669
I —

00:17:53.669 --> 00:17:54.669
Tom Sowell: Well, not necessarily.

00:17:54.669 --> 00:17:55.669
I would say, “the fall in the garden.”

00:17:55.669 --> 00:17:56.669
Ben Wattenberg: Right, okay.

00:17:56.669 --> 00:18:06.460
All right now, since the 1960s, the United
States of America has won the Cold War, remained

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:10.250
a — while a lot of people, including a lot
of — mostly conservatives were saying, “Oh,

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:11.410
America has lost its nerve.”

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:12.410
And we, because —

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:13.830
Tom Sowell: I don’t think it was mostly
conservatives.

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:23.070
Ben Wattenberg: Well, I recall an issue of
the Public Interest in the mid-1970s saying,

00:18:23.070 --> 00:18:28.080
“Has America lost its nerve,” with a whole
bunch of conservatives saying, “Oh, my God,

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:29.080
it’s terrible, we’re” —

00:18:29.080 --> 00:18:30.820
Tom Sowell: Well, this is Jimmy Carter’s
era.

00:18:30.820 --> 00:18:32.309
Ben Wattenberg: Well, maybe that’s right.

00:18:32.309 --> 00:18:39.150
But, so since this terrible event 30 years
ago, when the ’60s dropped upon us, we have

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:40.150
won the Cold War.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:45.490
We have continued to grow in affluence, in
professional skills.

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:48.800
We have absorbed about 20 million immigrants.

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:53.470
We are the wealthiest, freest country in the
history of the world.

00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:55.070
You would agree with all of that, roughly?

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:57.980
Tom Sowell: Well, I disagree entirely with
your base period.

00:18:57.980 --> 00:19:00.210
Ben Wattenberg: No, no, it’s your base period.

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:01.780
Tom Sowell: No, no.

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:03.350
No, no, no, no.

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:04.350
None of these things —

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:05.350
Ben Wattenberg: You established the 1960s.

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:06.350
I didn’t.

00:19:06.350 --> 00:19:07.350
I trapped you.

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:08.350
Tom Sowell: No, I’m saying that all those
things that you talk about began long before

00:19:08.350 --> 00:19:09.350
the 1960s.

00:19:09.350 --> 00:19:11.900
They faltered to a great extent under Jimmy
Carter.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:16.890
They were resumed with a renewed vigor in
the 1980s under an entirely different kind

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:17.890
of vision.

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:21.490
But I’m talking about the social decline
of the country because the social decline

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:24.179
is all the more striking because here is a
country that is prosperous.

00:19:24.179 --> 00:19:28.370
You can’t blame the crime rate on the fact
that there is more poverty.

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:29.640
There is less poverty.

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:31.320
There is more affluence.

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:35.669
It’s not due to foreigners because, as you
say, we’ve won the Cold War.

00:19:35.669 --> 00:19:38.650
All the normal things that you might blame
all this on aren’t there.

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:42.320
It’s not because of diseases because science
has conquered more diseases.

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:45.020
It’s all because of self-inflicted wounds.

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:48.710
And I’m saying these are the people who
inflicted those wounds, and this is why we

00:19:48.710 --> 00:19:50.390
shouldn’t listen to them anymore.

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:53.800
Ben Wattenberg: What are the nature of those
wounds?

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:54.800
Tom Sowell: Crime.

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:55.800
Ben Wattenberg: No, no, no.

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:56.800
I —

00:19:56.800 --> 00:20:00.900
Tom Sowell: The disintegration of the family,
the disintegration of the educational system.

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:04.510
And it’s not going to matter — we’ll
be like the man who gained the whole world

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:05.510
and lost his soul.

00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:10.750
Ben Wattenberg: Is there a common root to
all of those problems other than you heard

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:12.700
them from the anointed class?

00:20:12.700 --> 00:20:13.700
I mean, is there something —

00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:14.700
Tom Sowell: Yes, yes, yes.

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:17.350
Ben Wattenberg: Is it big government?

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:18.350
Is that what —

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:19.350
Tom Sowell: No, no.

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:25.429
It’s the notion that ordinary people cannot
be trusted to make the decisions that they’ve

00:20:25.429 --> 00:20:30.190
been making, that they must be preempted,
either by judges, in the case of crime, or

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:34.679
by the schools taking over the indoctrination
of other people’s children behind their

00:20:34.679 --> 00:20:41.100
back and against their protest, or what was
the other one?

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:44.210
The family.

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:49.049
Taking money from the taxpayers and subsidizing
behavior as well as encouraging it and legitimizing

00:20:49.049 --> 00:20:53.830
behavior that has turned out to be enormously
self-destructive, undermining the family in

00:20:53.830 --> 00:20:55.120
a thousand different ways.

00:20:55.120 --> 00:20:59.899
Ben Wattenberg: Tom, you write in your new
book, “The Vision of the Anointed,” available

00:20:59.899 --> 00:21:05.100
at all bookstores, about the “Teflon prophets.”

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:06.100
Tom Sowell: Yes.

00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:07.100
Ben Wattenberg: What is —

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:11.450
Tom Sowell: Well, I think my favorite is Paul
Ehrlich because he has been so consistently

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:13.090
wrong on so many things.

00:21:13.090 --> 00:21:16.929
One, predicting mass starvation, I think it
was in the ’70s or the ’80s.

00:21:16.929 --> 00:21:22.990
But predicting that we’re running out of
— running low on resources.

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:26.670
And Julian Simon made this famous bet with
him that he would offer to bet anybody a thousand

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:31.659
dollars that they could name a set of resources
and name a period of time, and at the end

00:21:31.659 --> 00:21:36.000
of that period of time, those resources would
not be more expensive, as they would be if

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:42.659
they were really running low, but would be
either stationary or falling in real terms.

00:21:42.659 --> 00:21:46.630
And Ehrlich rushed in with his list of 10
resources, and he decided we’d come back

00:21:46.630 --> 00:21:47.940
at the end of 10 years.

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:52.130
At the end of 10 years, not only was the bundle
of 10 resources cheaper in real terms than

00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:55.460
it was before, every single resource he named
was cheaper.

00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:58.679
Ben Wattenberg: Let me raise a final point
here.

00:21:58.679 --> 00:22:05.029
You make this vigorous attack in “The Vision
of the Anointed” that we should no longer

00:22:05.029 --> 00:22:06.220
listen to these people.

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:11.299
That’s sort of the basic — they’ve been
wrong, they don’t prove their points, it’s

00:22:11.299 --> 00:22:12.980
hurt us.

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:14.080
Who should we listen to?

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:17.260
Tom Sowell: We should listen first and foremost
to our own experience.

00:22:17.260 --> 00:22:21.950
You seem to be saying, well, there must be
alternative saviors.

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:23.860
We should stop looking for saviors.

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:29.490
I mean, the society has not existed for thousands
of years because it had a succession of saviors.

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:33.929
It’s existed because it has institutions
and processes through which people can realize

00:22:33.929 --> 00:22:34.929
their own goals.

00:22:34.929 --> 00:22:40.110
Ben Wattenberg: No, I understand that, but
you are attacking people who would like to

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:42.640
lead us and tell us how the world works.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:43.640
Tom Sowell: No, no.

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:45.549
No, no, they don’t want to tell us how the
world works.

00:22:45.549 --> 00:22:48.110
They want to take over the decision for us.

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:52.580
They don’t tell parents how they ought to
teach sex education to their children.

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:56.390
They put this material in the schools behind
the backs of the parents with instructions

00:22:56.390 --> 00:22:57.572
not to let the parents see it.

00:22:57.572 --> 00:22:58.572
That’s the problem.

00:22:58.572 --> 00:23:03.520
Ben Wattenberg: Okay, but we live in an open
and democratic society.

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:11.309
Now, Mr. and Mrs. John Q. Public are listening
to this program, I hope, and they’re saying,

00:23:11.309 --> 00:23:13.789
“Boy, that guy really makes a lot of sense.”

00:23:13.789 --> 00:23:18.590
But basically, it’s been a negative proposition:
Don’t listen to these people, don’t do

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:19.590
this, don’t do that.

00:23:19.590 --> 00:23:20.590
Tom Sowell: No, no.

00:23:20.590 --> 00:23:21.590
Ben Wattenberg: Who should —

00:23:21.590 --> 00:23:22.590
Tom Sowell: Don’t let those people run your
lives.

00:23:22.590 --> 00:23:23.610
Ben Wattenberg: Okay, but how do you practically
—

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:26.299
Tom Sowell: I think it’s a non-problem,
Ben.

00:23:26.299 --> 00:23:27.309
It’s a non-problem.

00:23:27.309 --> 00:23:29.159
Those people are adults just like you and
me.

00:23:29.159 --> 00:23:32.450
They have been running their lives for thousands
of years.

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:35.150
They don’t need me to tell them what to
do.

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:36.440
Ben Wattenberg: But this is a —

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:39.450
Tom Sowell: It’s not a question I want to
take over from people on the left.

00:23:39.450 --> 00:23:42.909
I want them to continue to make their decisions
as they see fit.

00:23:42.909 --> 00:23:46.179
Ben Wattenberg: Right, but this is a democracy.

00:23:46.179 --> 00:23:47.179
They have to vote.

00:23:47.179 --> 00:23:49.330
Tom Sowell: Even in a democracy, they can
live their lives as they see fit.

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:50.899
Ben Wattenberg: Who should they vote for?

00:23:50.899 --> 00:23:52.750
Tom Sowell: Oh, good heavens.

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:56.029
They’ve been voting for whoever they wanted
to without my help for 200 years.

00:23:56.029 --> 00:24:00.010
Ben Wattenberg: But some people are voting
for the people who are listening to these

00:24:00.010 --> 00:24:01.170
anointed people.

00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:03.370
I mean, give the audience some advice.

00:24:03.370 --> 00:24:05.240
Tell them what to do.

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:10.510
Tom Sowell: Well, last year, in 1994, without
any help from me whatsoever, they changed

00:24:10.510 --> 00:24:14.760
who they voted for, and so it’s a non-problem.

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:20.179
Ben Wattenberg: You say without any help from
you, but you and your confreres in the conservative

00:24:20.179 --> 00:24:22.830
movement I think have had an influence.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:25.670
Tom Sowell: But it wasn’t because of this
book, obviously.

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:29.330
Ben Wattenberg: Not because of this book,
but because of your earlier wonderful books.

00:24:29.330 --> 00:24:32.200
Tom Sowell, thank you very much.

00:24:32.200 --> 00:24:34.160
And thank you.

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:41.920
